'''Medieval Southwestern Kaftia''' covers the period from the founding of the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] in 403 AD to the defeat of the [[Lake Confederation]] circa 1180. The era is defined by the interaction and eventual collision of several distinct political traditions: the expansionist [[Kingdom of Burawa]] on the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] coast, the declining [[Kingdom of Ufyen]] in the highlands, the agricultural [[Kingdom of Sago]] in the western lowlands, the council-based [[Lake Confederation]] around the [[Great Kaftian Lake]], the chiefdoms and trading settlements of the [[Western Kaftian Tradition|Western]] coast, and the consolidating [[Kingdom of Orma]] in the eastern highlands.

The period opened with the collapse of the [[Mesogean League]], which disrupted the trade networks that had sustained the southern coastal cities, and closed with the [[Kingdom of Burawa]]'s absorption of nearly the entire peninsula. In between, the medieval centuries saw the arrival of [[Christianity]], the settlement of the [[Sabins]] in [[Sabia]], the formation of new states and the destruction of old ones, and the emergence of the [[Hoggaanka]] feudal system. By 1180, the only independent states remaining were the [[Kingdom of Shedou]] and the [[Kingdom of Orma]].

== The Kingdom of Burawa ==
{{main|Medieval History of Burawa}}

[[File:Bugad_Appa_Marriage.jpg|thumb|right|Artist's depiction of the union of [[Republic of Mazar|Mazar]] and [[Kingdom of Iskhal|Iskhal]] in 403 AD, founding the [[Kingdom of Burawa]].]]

The [[Kingdom of Burawa]] was founded in 403 AD when '''[[Bugad the Great|Bugad I]]''' of the [[Urtog Dynasty]] of [[Kingdom of Iskhal|Iskhal]] married '''[[Appa of Mazar]]''' of the [[Mazar Clique|House of Masar]], uniting the two coastal city-states in response to the economic crisis caused by the [[Mesogean League]]'s collapse. Over the following eight centuries, the kingdom expanded from its coastal base to control nearly the entire peninsula through a sequence of conquests:

* '''[[Dagaalka Galbeed]]''' (~430–450): Absorption of the [[Kingdom of Galbeed]]
* '''[[Dagaalka Sago]]''' (~700s): Military conquest of the [[Kingdom of Sago]], transforming Burawa into a territorial power
* '''[[Northern Conquest of Burawa|Northern Conquest]]''' (893–925): Destruction of the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]], ending two millennia of highland independence
* '''[[Dagaalka Fayeha]]''' (~900s–1000s): Administrative absorption of the [[Western Kaftian Tradition|Western Tradition]]
* '''[[Dagaalka Harada]]''' (~1100s–1180): Decades-long land campaign defeating the [[Lake Confederation]]

The kingdom was governed by five royal houses — the [[Urtog Dynasty]], the [[Mazar Clique|House of Masar]], [[House Kadim]] (twice), and [[House Barkhad]] — with 31 kings across the period (see '''[[List of kings of Burawa]]'''). The adoption of [[Christianity]] under '''[[Iacob I]]''' (573), the development of the [[Hoggaanka]] feudal system (1105), and the deliberate policy of '''[[Burawisation]]''' reshaped the peninsula's cultural landscape.

== The Kingdom of Ufyen ==
{{main|Saleebaan Period}}

The [[Kingdom of Ufyen]], which had dominated the southern highlands since the [[Geedi Period]] (~1,000 BCE), entered its final era — the '''[[Saleebaan Period]]''' — in response to the founding of the [[Kingdom of Burawa]]. '''[[Dhegdheer]]''', commander of a ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' garrison in the [[Kushish]] highlands, seized power from the declining [[Warfaa Period|Liibaan Dynasty]] around 400 AD, founding the Saleebaan Dynasty.

The Saleebaan Period encompassed four dynasties over five centuries:

{| class="wikitable"
! Dynasty !! Dates !! Character
|-
| '''Saleebaan''' || ~400–550 || ''Kaas''-lord family. Maintained garrisons, tariffs at [[Jowhar]], and the [[Waaq]] state cult.
|-
| '''Rooble''' || ~550–700 || Agricultural focus. Expanded [[Lake Haro]] irrigation. Relative stabilisation.
|-
| '''Samatar''' || ~700–800 || Decline. Lost outer ''kaas'' forts to Burawa's encroachment on the [[Buran River]] corridor.
|-
| '''Guri''' || ~800–925 || Final dynasty. '''[[Hayaan]]''', last king, fell during the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa|Northern Conquest]].
|}

Throughout the period, the Kingdom of Ufyen contracted steadily. By the time of the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa|Northern Conquest]] (893–925), the kingdom had been reduced to its [[Lake Haro]] and [[Kushish]] core — a fraction of the territory it had controlled at its [[Geedi Period]] height. The fall of [[Badalga-Jir]] and the surrender of [[Hayaan]] ended nearly two millennia of continuous highland rule. The [[Buur Waaq]] summit shrine, centre of the [[Waaq]] state religion since the founding of Ufyen, was abandoned after the conquest and never converted to a Christian site.

== The Kingdom of Sago ==
{{main|Kingdom of Sago}}

The [[Kingdom of Sago]], formed around 100 BCE when the [[Kingdom of Dafar]] conquered the [[Kingdom of Hooyal]], was the most '''populous''' state on the peninsula during the early medieval period. Its territory encompassed the fertile [[Dafar River|Dafar]] and [[Hooyal River|Hooyal]] valleys — the richest agricultural land in [[Southwestern Kaftia]] — and its large farming population gave it considerable political weight.

Sago's weakness was '''iron poverty'''. The kingdom had no local iron deposits and depended on imports from the [[Kushish]] highlands (through the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]]) and from the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] trade (through the coastal cities). This dependence left Sago vulnerable to economic pressure and militarily outmatched against iron-armed opponents.

The kingdom maintained its independence through the early medieval centuries, forming the third leg of the three-bloc system alongside [[Kingdom of Burawa|Burawa]] and [[Kingdom of Ufyen|Ufyen]]. Sago's agricultural surplus made it a critical trading partner for both rivals. Internally, the kingdom remained a primarily agricultural society organised around the river-valley farming communities that had existed since the [[Ancient Southwestern Kaftia|Ancient period]], with local chiefs governing under the authority of a central king.

The '''[[Dagaalka Sago]]''' (~700s) ended Sago's independence. '''[[Guled I]]''' of Burawa conquered the kingdom through a direct military campaign; Sago's numerical advantage could not compensate for its lack of iron weapons and its limited military organisation. The [[Dafar River|Dafar]] and [[Hooyal River|Hooyal]] valleys became the breadbasket of the Burawi empire, and the large Sago population was the first major target of '''[[Burawisation]]'''.

== The Lake Confederation ==
{{main|Lake Confederation}}

=== Formation and structure ===

The '''[[Lake Confederation]]''' was the dominant political entity of the [[Northern Kaftian Tradition|Northern Basin]] throughout the medieval period. A loose political union of approximately ten to fifteen chiefdoms around the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] and in the surrounding savanna, the Confederation emerged in the late [[Ancient Southwestern Kaftia|Ancient period]] as a collective response to Ufyen's control of the mountain passes and the resulting scarcity of metal goods in the north.

The Confederation's member chiefdoms were drawn from the [[Dakari people|Dakari]], [[Tukulor people|Tukulor]], [[Lebu people|Lebu]], and [[Sarafoni people|Sarafoni]] peoples. Governance was council-based: hereditary chiefs met periodically to resolve disputes, set trade policy, and coordinate military responses. The paramount chief — the '''[[Lamido]]''' — was a rotating position that passed between the major chiefdoms on a fixed schedule, preventing any single chiefdom from dominating the others.

=== Economy and society ===

The Confederation's economy rested on three pillars: '''agriculture''' (sorghum and millet cultivation in the savanna), '''cattle herding''' (pastoralism across the grasslands, with cattle serving as both wealth and status markers), and '''lake fishing''' (the [[Great Kaftian Lake]]'s enormous fish stocks supporting lakeside communities). Trade networks linked the lakeside chiefdoms to the [[Western Kaftian Tradition|Western coast]] via [[Fayeha]], bypassing the Ufyen- and later Burawa-controlled mountain passes.

Society was stratified. A nobility of hereditary chiefs and their extended families governed each chiefdom. Below them, specialist castes — smiths, leatherworkers, and the '''[[Northern Kaftian Tradition#Oral tradition|gewel]]''' bardic caste — held distinct social positions. The farming and herding majority formed the base of the social order. This stratified social order was characteristic of the savanna and lakeland societies of northern [[Kaftia]].

=== Religion ===

The Confederation's religious life was centred on '''[[Ndogalu]]''' ("Faith of the Water's Origin"), an organised tradition that regarded the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] as a sacred entity inhabited by powerful spirits. Ritual offerings were made at lakeside shrines, and the spirits of the lake were invoked for rain, fish harvests, and protection from storms. Each chiefdom also maintained its own local nature spirits — river spirits, tree spirits, and the Earth Mother associated with soil fertility — creating a diverse religious landscape united by the shared framework of Ndogalu and its reverence for the lake.

[[Christianity]] did not penetrate the northern basin in any significant way during the medieval period. Full Christianisation of the north was an [[Imperial Southwestern Kaftia|Imperial-era]] project.

=== Military ===

The Confederation had no standing army. In times of conflict, each chief called up '''mobilised levies''' from his chiefdom's farming and herding population. Warriors fought with spears, shields, and bows; iron weapons were scarcer than in the south due to the north's limited access to the [[Kushish]] mines. The Confederation's military strength lay in the coordination of multiple chiefdoms' levies and in the defenders' knowledge of the terrain — advantages that made the [[Dagaalka Harada]] a grinding, decades-long campaign rather than a swift conquest.

=== Fall ===

The [[Dagaalka Harada]] (~1100s–1180) was fought entirely overland. The Confederation initially resisted as a coordinated unit, but as Burawa's professional army and [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] levies advanced, the alliance fragmented. Individual chiefdoms cut deals or surrendered, and Burawa subdued them one by one. '''[[Lamido]] Yero''', the last paramount chief, surrendered circa 1180. Burawa also took the [[Dakari people|Dakari]] coastline east to the [[Ayorin Rainforest]].

== The Kingdom of Shedou ==
{{main|Kingdom of Shedou}}

The '''[[Kingdom of Shedou]]''' was a [[Sarafoni people|Sarafoni]] kingdom that '''broke away''' from the [[Lake Confederation]] between approximately 1000 and 1100 AD. Its founder, '''[[Buur Ndiadiane]]''' ("the Uniter"), rejected the Confederation's rotating [[Lamido]] system in favour of a centralised monarchy — the '''Buur''' (king) holding permanent authority.

The breakaway was driven by two factors: frustration with the Confederation's inability to mount a unified response to Burawi expansion, and Shedou's geographic exclusion from the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] fisheries and lakeside trade networks. The kingdom was situated near modern [[Sarfone]], on the northwestern coast, with access to the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] but not to the lake.

Shedou survived the fall of the Lake Confederation (~1180), remaining as the last independent state on the peninsula under its final ruler, '''[[Buur Lat Sukaabe]]'''. Its defeat circa 1200 marks the transition to the [[Imperial Southwestern Kaftia|Imperial]] period.

== The Western Coast ==
{{main|Western Kaftian Tradition}}

The communities of the [[Western Kaftian Tradition]] — the [[Fayehan people|Fayehan]] and [[Kijuran people|Kijuran]] peoples of [[Fayeha]], [[Kijura]], and [[Wolonia]] — remained organised in '''chiefdoms and small coastal trading settlements''' throughout the medieval period. No centralised state emerged on the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] coast comparable to the kingdoms of the south or the Lake Confederation of the north.

The Western communities played an important role as '''trade intermediaries''', linking the [[Lake Confederation]] to the sea and enabling northern goods to bypass the Burawa-controlled mountain passes. The [[Cowrie|cowrie shell]] trade — cowries being the primary currency of the northern basin — passed through Fayehan hands. This maritime trade route was the principal economic connection between the Northern and Western traditions.

Western society retained '''{{W|matrilineality|matrilineal}}''' social structures — descent traced through the female line — which distinguished it from the patriarchal societies of both the south and the north. When the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] absorbed the Western communities during the '''[[Dagaalka Fayeha]]''' (~900s–1000s), these matrilineal practices proved more resistant to [[Burawisation]] than the highland [[Waaq]] cult, persisting in [[Fayeha]] and [[Kijura]] well into the Imperial period.

== The Kingdom of Orma ==
{{main|Kingdom of Orma}}

The medieval period saw the consolidation of the [[Orma people|Orma]] peoples of the eastern highlands from a collection of small chiefdoms into a unified '''[[Kingdom of Orma]]'''. This process was catalysed by the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa|Northern Conquest]] (893–925): one Orma king allied with [[Kingdom of Burawa|Burawa]] against their mutual enemy, the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]], and used the prestige and spoils of the alliance to consolidate authority over the other Orma chiefdoms.

After the fall of Ufyen, the relationship between Burawa and Orma fluctuated between alliance and rivalry. Border disputes over [[Maianka]] and [[Sabia]] were a recurring source of conflict; the '''[[First Orma-Burawa War]]''' (1204) saw the [[Kingdom of Orma]] briefly capture [[Badalga]].

The Kingdom of Orma converted to [[Christianity]] '''independently''' of the [[Burawi Catholic Church]], through its own [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] trade contacts along the southeastern coast. This shared faith deepened Orma's diplomatic ties with Mesogean states, who supported Orma as a '''buffer state''' against Burawi expansion. Marriage alliances between Orma and Mesogean royal houses reinforced this dynamic — a pattern of international balance-of-power politics that persisted into the modern era.

== The Sabins of Sabia ==

The '''[[Sabins]]''', a Riphean people displaced during the collapse of the [[Mesogean League]], crossed the [[Mesogean Sea]] and settled in [[Sabia]] by approximately 438 AD. They brought [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] culture, [[Christianity]], and administrative traditions directly to southeastern [[Southwestern Kaftia]], forming part of the same wave of Christian contact that reached the coastal cities through [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] traders.

The Sabins were eventually assimilated into Burawi culture, but [[Sabia]] became a distinctive '''[[Hoggaanka|Hogan]]''' ruled by Sabin-descended families — one of the earliest and most culturally distinctive Hogas, retaining a Mesogean-Riphean character within a Burawi framework. Sabia's frontier position between [[Kingdom of Burawa|Burawa]] and the [[Kingdom of Orma]] made it a frequent flashpoint in border disputes between the two powers.

== Christianity ==
{{main|Burawi Catholic Church}}

[[Christianity]] arrived on the [[Southwestern Kaftia|Southwestern Kaftian]] peninsula through [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] merchants and monks in the early 6th century, spreading first among the urban merchant class of the southern coast. The conversion of '''[[Iacob I]]''' of Burawa (573) established the '''[[Burawi Catholic Church]]''' as a state institution. The [[Kingdom of Orma]] converted independently through its own Mesogean contacts.

The spread of Christianity displaced the [[Waaq]] state religion of the south gradually on the coast, forcibly in the highlands after the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa|Northern Conquest]] (925), and barely at all in the northern basin, where [[Ndogalu]] and local nature-spirit traditions persisted throughout the medieval period. Full Christianisation of the north was an [[Imperial Southwestern Kaftia|Imperial-era]] project.

== Economy and trade ==

The collapse of the [[Mesogean League]] (~300–500 AD) disrupted trans-Mesogean trade and was a major driver of political consolidation across the peninsula. The southern coast gradually rebuilt its commercial networks with new Mesogean partners — [[Volisanians|Volisania]], [[Berona]], and other successor states of the Terdolian Peninsula.

The peninsula's economic geography was shaped by three resource zones:
* '''The coast''': [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] maritime trade (iron exports, luxury imports)
* '''The western lowlands''': Agriculture (the [[Dafar River|Dafar]] and [[Hooyal River|Hooyal]] valleys as the peninsula's breadbasket)
* '''The highlands''': [[Kushish]] iron mines (the peninsula's strategic resource)
* '''The northern basin''': Agriculture, cattle, and [[Great Kaftian Lake]] fisheries; [[Cowrie|cowrie shell]] currency; trade routed through the [[Western Kaftian Tradition|Western coast]] to bypass the mountains

Slavery was significant throughout the peninsula. In the south, forms included war captives, the trans-Mesogean slave trade, debt bondage, and domestic servitude. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] moderated the institution — prohibiting the enslavement of Christians and imposing treatment standards — without abolishing it.

== Society ==

The medieval period saw increasing divergence between the '''literate, Christian, urbanising south''' and the '''oral, traditional, rural north''' — a division that the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] reinforced geographically.

In the south, the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] operated monastic schools and the crown maintained a royal academy; the [[Burawi language]] developed a literary tradition in the [[Mesogean script|Echian script]]; and Mesogean-Kaftian architectural, musical, and artistic traditions flourished. The deliberate policy of '''[[Burawisation]]''' — imposing Burawi language, Christianity, Burawi law, and Burawi settlers on conquered populations — gradually homogenised the southern territories.

In the north, the '''[[Northern Kaftian Tradition#Oral tradition|gewel]]''' bardic caste preserved oral histories and genealogies; w centred religious life on the [[Great Kaftian Lake]]; and social structures based on hereditary chiefs, specialist castes, and mobilised levies persisted without significant external influence until the [[Dagaalka Harada]] brought Burawi conquest in the late 12th century.

Regional variation persisted even within Burawa's borders. The [[Western Kaftian Tradition|Western Tradition]] retained {{W|matrilineality|matrilineal}} social practices. The [[Sabins]] of [[Sabia]] preserved a Mesogean-Riphean cultural character. The Ufyen highlands kept [[Waaq]] in folk practice beneath a Christian overlay.

== Disease ==

A major plague reached [[Southwestern Kaftia]] via [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] trade routes in the early-to-mid 7th century. The epidemic struck the southern coastal port cities with moderate severity, disrupting urban commerce and causing significant population loss. The interior and the northern basin were partially insulated by distance from the trade routes.

